INT. OPERATING THEATER 4 - NIGHT

Cold white light. The steady beep of a monitor. A patient lies draped on the table, only a rectangle of skin exposed.

DR. KALANI MAHOE, 50s, surgeon, bends over the field. BEATRIX LUND, 30s, scrub nurse, passes instruments. TOMASZ WRONA, 20s, the resident, holds a retractor. YARA HADDAD, 40s, circulating nurse, stands by the door. At the head of the table, the ANAESTHETIST, 40s, watches the monitors.

MAHOE
Retractor.

TOMASZ
Got it.

MAHOE
(to the room)
Everyone with me. We are closing in ten minutes.

ANAESTHETIST
Blood pressure's holding.

BEATRIX
Suture.

MAHOE
(to himself)
Almost there.

The anaesthetist glances at the screen, then at the surgeon. She taps a dial.

ROSA
Kalani, her sats just dropped.

MAHOE
How far?

ROSA
Ninety-one.

TOMASZ
Is that bad?

YARA
(from the door)
Don't ask.

MAHOE
Rosa, ventilate. Beatrix, hold the sutures.

BEATRIX
Holding.

MAHOE
Tomasz, you can breathe now.

Tomasz lets out a long breath. The monitor settles.
